---
import GuideLayout from '../components/GuideLayout.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import StatusBadge from '../components/StatusBadge.astro';
import { FAQS } from '../data/game';
import { GAME_IMAGES } from '../lib/images';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Demo guide', path: '/demo/' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'what-is-demo', label: 'What the demo is' },
  { id: 'access', label: 'How to access it' },
  { id: 'included', label: 'What is included' },
  { id: 'route', label: 'A practical first route' },
  { id: 'co-op', label: 'Co-op in the demo' },
  { id: 'saves', label: 'Saves and limits' },
  { id: 'tip-notes', label: 'Field notes' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'Demo questions' },
];

const faq = FAQS.demo;
---

<GuideLayout
  seoTitle="Main Sequence Demo Guide: Content, Co-op & Save Status"
  description="What's in the Main Sequence demo? Confirmed content, the October co-op update, demo limits, save-status unknowns and a practical route through the early technology tiers."
  eyebrow="Demo field guide"
  title="Main Sequence demo guide: what is playable now"
  intro="The free Steam demo is the most useful reference point before Early Access. Here is what it actually contains, what changed in October, and which questions are still open."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  toc={toc}
  sourceIds={[
    'steam-demo',
    'steam-news-2026-10-19',
    'steam-news-2026-10-21',
    'steam-news-2026-05-28',
    'steam-news-2026-06-09',
    'steam-news-2026-09-09',
    'steam-forum-logistics',
    'steam-news-2026-09-21',
  ]}
  image={GAME_IMAGES['main-sequence-conveyor-belt-build.jpg']}
  imageAlt="A Main Sequence player builds a line of Conveyor Belt Mk. 1 segments inside a station"
  imageCaption="Belt construction is a research-gated part of the current demo progression. Official Steam screenshot."
  meta={['Free on Steam', 'First two tech tiers', 'Co-op added October 19', 'Demo saves: unconfirmed']}
  faq={faq}
  pageType="WebPage"
  dataNote="The demo changes between patches. This page reflects the October 21, 2026 hotfix and is not a substitute for the in-game tutorial."
>
  <section id="what-is-demo" class="scroll-mt-28">
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="callout-title">Current status</span>
      <p class="m-0 text-fog">
        The Main Sequence demo is a free, separate Steam app. It was released on
        May 28, 2026 and remains listed as of our November 1 check. The October
        19 Next Fest update added the new tech tree, Electromagnetic Science,
        new equipment and places, the Shield Generator Mk. 1 and co-op
        multiplayer. A small hotfix followed on October 21.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      Steam describes the demo as a slice of the starting star system, covering
      the first two technology tiers and culminating in the Slipstream Drive.
      The October update extended the demo’s research options for players who
      reached the previous endpoint, so “first two tiers” is still a useful
      summary but no longer the complete patch story.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a development demo, not a content-complete preview of the full
      game. Later systems, the Trading Outpost and late-game science featured in
      the September playtest are playtest material unless an official update
      explicitly says they are in the demo.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="access" class="scroll-mt-28">
    <h2>How to access the demo</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Open the <a href={SITE.game.demoSteam} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Main Sequence Demo page on Steam</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Sign in to Steam and select the demo download. It is a separate app from
        the full game, so searching only for the main store page can make the
        demo button easy to miss.
      </li>
      <li>
        Install, launch and follow the in-game tutorial. The demo opens in the
        starting asteroid belt with the mining ship.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      A Steam account is required. We do not host or mirror the demo files. The
      demo’s availability after Steam Next Fest is controlled by the developer;
      it was still listed at the time of our last check.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="included" class="scroll-mt-28">
    <h2>What is included in the demo</h2>

    <div class="data-table-wrap not-prose mt-5">
      <table class="data-table data-table-stack">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">System</th>
            <th scope="col">Status in demo</th>
            <th scope="col">Detail</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="System">Starting star system</td>
            <td data-label="Status in demo"><StatusBadge status="confirmed" /></td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Detail">Steam frames the demo around the starting system, not the whole galaxy.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="System">Mining and ship refining</td>
            <td data-label="Status in demo"><StatusBadge status="confirmed" /></td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Detail">Scan, mine and refine aboard the starting ship.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="System">Station building and factories</td>
            <td data-label="Status in demo"><StatusBadge status="confirmed" /></td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Detail">Build a station, place machines and start automating production.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="System">Research / Slipstream Drive</td>
            <td data-label="Status in demo"><StatusBadge status="confirmed" /></td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Detail">Steam names Slipstream Drive as the early demo goal. Only Warp Fuel can be inserted into it.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="System">Electromagnetic Science</td>
            <td data-label="Status in demo"><StatusBadge status="confirmed" label="Added Oct 19" /></td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Detail">Added for players who reached the end of the previous demo content.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="System">Combat and threat defense</td>
            <td data-label="Status in demo"><StatusBadge status="confirmed" /></td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Detail">The demo includes enemies and the October update added a shield generator.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="System">Co-op</td>
            <td data-label="Status in demo"><StatusBadge status="confirmed" label="Added Oct 19" /></td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Detail">Multiplayer was added to the demo. The store page’s older single-player wording is out of date.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="System">Full game progression</td>
            <td data-label="Status in demo"><StatusBadge status="unknown" label="Not included" /></td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Detail">Later systems and late-game science belong to the full/playtest build, not the public demo.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-4 text-xs leading-relaxed text-mist">
      The demo is a moving target. It received a visual overhaul before release
      and multiple hotfixes afterward. Treat exact recipes and costs as
      patch-dependent.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="route" class="scroll-mt-28">
    <h2>A practical first route</h2>
    <p>
      The demo’s tutorial gives you a sequence of missions. The following route
      is a synthesis of the official demo description and a community-tested
      account of the early progression. It is not a scripted walkthrough with
      hidden puzzle solutions.
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Scan and mine.</strong> Learn how the ship scanner identifies
        resource asteroids, then mine enough material to test the refining loop.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Use the ship workstation.</strong> You do not need a complete
        station before you can process ore. Early crafting happens on the ship.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Build the first station room.</strong> The Command module is the
        station-design entry point. Plan for power before you fill the room with
        machines.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Power the station.</strong> This is the step that trips up new
        players. A docked ship does not necessarily solve station power in the
        current demo; the community-tested answer is to build station-side
        generation and supply it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Queue Logistics I.</strong> The official demo screenshot shows
        Logistics I in the research queue at 0/10. Conveyor belts are not
        available until this research step is handled.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Build a belt loop.</strong> Once belts are available, connect
        one machine to storage and watch the item flow. Add a splitter only when
        you need to send material to two destinations.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Automate mining, then scale research.</strong> Mining Drones
        follows Logistics I in the visible tree. That is the transition from
        hand-feeding to a genuinely automated base.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Push toward Slipstream Drive.</strong> Build a stable science
        loop before chasing the drive. The demo’s endpoint is a system-travel
        technology, not a fully upgraded empire.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <Figure
      name="main-sequence-production-lines.jpg"
      alt="Multiple orange production machines and blue-lit conveyor belts arranged on a Main Sequence station floor"
      caption="A production floor is easier to expand when machine rows, belts and storage have room around them."
      sizes="(min-width: 768px) 760px, 100vw"
      class="my-6"
    />

    <div class="callout callout-warning">
      <span class="callout-title">Tutorial gap warning</span>
      <p class="m-0 text-sm">
        Demo players have reported that the tutorial does not clearly explain
        the station-power step or how to reach conveyor belts. The step-by-step
        fix is covered in our{' '}
        <a href="/guides/first-station-logistics/">conveyor belt guide</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="co-op" class="scroll-mt-28">
    <h2>Co-op in the demo</h2>
    <p>
      The October 19 update added co-op to the demo. In the full game and the
      demo’s design, each player is the captain of their own ship. The official
      examples suggest splitting logistics, defense and exploration, but the
      game does not force those roles.
    </p>
    <p>
      What is still unknown: the maximum player count, dedicated-server
      support, host migration, cross-play and exactly how shared progression
      works. The demo is the current test bed, not a final specification.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use the <a href="/guides/co-op/#crew-planner">co-op guide’s crew
      planner</a> to assign a first shift across two to four captains.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="saves" class="scroll-mt-28">
    <h2>Will demo progress carry over?</h2>
    <p>
      This has not been confirmed. No official statement says demo saves,
      stations or research will transfer to Early Access. Treat the demo as a
      preview, not an early-access save file.
    </p>
    <p>
      The safest preservation strategy is to save useful blueprints rather than
      assuming a full save transfer. Whether blueprints themselves survive the
      version boundary is also unconfirmed, so keep screenshots or notes of
      designs you care about.
    </p>

    <h3>What could change at launch</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Recipe costs, research requirements and module balance can change
        between the demo and Early Access.
      </li>
      <li>
        The demo’s co-op performance was still being optimized during the
        September playtest and October Next Fest.
      </li>
      <li>
        The game is entering Early Access, so the launch build is not the final
        1.0 balance either.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="tip-notes" class="scroll-mt-28">
    <h2>Field notes for a cleaner demo run</h2>
    <h3>Power before production</h3>
    <p>
      A factory line that cannot run is just expensive decoration. Build and
      fuel station power before committing floor space to a chain of machines.
      The community-reported tutorial gap makes this the first thing to verify.
    </p>
    <h3>Research is a production input</h3>
    <p>
      Science is not a passive XP bar. Certain produced items are converted into
      research through Science Labs. That means your research pace is part of
      your factory design: if science stalls, check the supply chain before
      assuming the game is broken.
    </p>
    <h3>Read the queue</h3>
    <p>
      The research tree exposes prerequisites. Technology names that seem
      missing may be later nodes, or may require a different ore to be
      researched first. The demo’s tutorial has known gaps; the tree is a more
      reliable map than a single mission line.
    </p>
    <h3>Don’t chase a finished “best build” yet</h3>
    <p>
      The current build is a demo and the launch build is Early Access. Recipes
      and costs have already changed in patches. Use layouts that are easy to
      modify, and avoid copying numeric ratios from a build that may no longer
      match the current version.
    </p>

    <div class="panel p-5 mt-7 not-prose">
      <p class="font-mono text-[0.59rem] uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-signal">Related tool</p>
      <h3 class="mt-2 font-display text-2xl font-semibold uppercase text-paper">Track your demo run</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-mist">
        The demo checklist turns this route into a progress list saved in your
        browser, from requirement checks to co-op and the Slipstream Drive
        objective.
      </p>
      <a href="/tools/demo-checklist/" class="arrow-link mt-4">Open the demo checklist</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
